  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 104 PM MDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nA weak \"wave\" will lift north across eastern Idaho \nthrough tomorrow morning, bringing an increase in showers and \nthunderstorms including a few storms overnight. The best chance of \nrain will be generally east of an Oakley to Island Park line.\nWe may see a few light showers to the northwest of that, but chances \nare currently under 20%. We could see some brief heavier downpours \nfarther east, especially outside of the Snake Plain.\nThere is a 10-50% chance of gusts over 35 mph, with high end \napproaching 45 mph. By tomorrow afternoon, the next round of showers \nand storms will already be developing. The bulk of this round will \ninitially forming across the central mountains south through the \nMagic Valley and South Hills/Albion Mountains.\nExpect some heavy downpours and also a 40-90% for gusts over 35 mph, \npushing 50-55 mph. This shifts quickly to the northeast tomorrow \nnight. As low pressure along the coast, another \"wave\" races north \non Wednesday. This will keep off another round of showers and \nthunderstorms, with the main focus being across the central \nmountains. There is a 10-40% chance of gusts over 35 mph as current \ntiming pushes any risk of strong/severe storms into Montana. A \ncouple of higher resolution models have isolated storms east of I-15 \nand around Island Park, but we currently have that potential under \n10% in the forecast. The bigger issue will likely be increasing \nwinds across the entire forecast area.\nThe strongest winds would be across portions of the central \nmountains, Arco Desert/INL and along the Montana border. We pushed \nthe Blend of Models forecast toward the highest end of the range \nbased on trends and the pattern, which puts us close to WIND \nADVISORY thresholds. Temperatures will remain in the upper 70s to \nmid 80s for highs again Tuesday, but drop back into 70s, even \nmid/upper 60s for higher valleys with the front on Wednesday. \n\nBeyond that, general low pressure settles in along the coast into \nearly next week. Initially, showers and storms will basically\ndevelop across the central mountains. Heading into the weekend,\nshowers and storms will become more widespread. The amount of\nmonsoon moisture does increase, so the potential for heavier\nrainfall may return. Of note, although the main GFS models is an\nextreme outlier, it wants to be aggressive with grabbing the\nremnants of tropical development in the Pacific and pull it\nnorth across Idaho. All other models (ECMWF and it ensembles,\nthe GEFS and AIGFS) do not show this at all. Either way,\ntemperatures look to remain cooler with highs in the 60s and\n70s.",
